usually car salesmen are thought to be  the slimiest and most manipulative  people in the world aside from using  pushy sales tactics and predatory  financing car salesmen often don't mind  selling you a piece of trash in fact  they'll probably do it with a smile  considering this i don't think you'd be  surprised to hear that 87 of american  adults dislike something about the car  buying process also if you're one of the  few people who had a positive experience  many would argue that you got taken  advantage of so much that you didn't  even realize but one guy named bob  brockman actually took advantage of car  dealerships so hard that they didn't  even realize throughout his career bob  sold overpriced software and hardware to  dealerships across the nation often with  multi-decade-long contracts and after he  was done milking the dealerships bob  turned around and tried to milk the  government but this didn't work out too  well as he was charged with the largest  tax evasion charge in history so here's  how bob brockman made billions by taking  advantage of dealerships and the  government and how he eventually got  caught  taking a look back bob brockman was born  in 1941 in saint petersburg florida this  was just before the us joined world war  ii so bob was born at the height of the  war but this wasn't too bad for bob as  the war would come to an end by the time  he turned five anyway his dad owned a  gas station and his mom worked as a  physiotherapist so he had a pretty  comfortable life financially this  allowed him to focus on his education  and excel in school in fact he graduated  samakumlade which is the highest  distinction from the university of  florida with a business degree in 1963.  bob was also a member of the  university's business honor society so  there's no doubt that he was a pretty  sharp guy out of college bob got a  marketing job at ford while also serving  in the us marine reserves this didn't  last long though as bob switched over to  ibm in 1966 and this is where he really  got his foundation for selling bob was  tasked with the job of selling ibm's  mainframe computer services to car  dealers and out of the gate bob was a  star performer maybe it was his young  ambition or his time at ford we'll  probably never know but it seemed like  bob was born to sell being the star that  he was bob knew that he didn't really  need ibm so in 1970 he left the company  and launched his own company called  universal computer services during his  time at ibm bob had dealt with dozens of  car dealerships and he knew exactly what  they wanted less paperwork so he taught  himself how to code and developed a  piece of software that could synthesize  multiple financial statements into one  for people who owned multiple car  dealerships this was a godsend soon  enough bob expanded his software to help  dealers with managing inventory  generating weekly reports and tracking  sales and the car dealerships ate this  up bob leaned into this business over  the next 20 years and he got quite rich  as he made hundreds of millions up until  this point bob hadn't really done  anything slimy per se sure he might have  used some shady sales tactics when  selling to dealerships but for the most  part bob was simply offering an  extremely valuable service to dealers  and he was rewarded quite handsomely his  next business move however wasn't nearly  as moral  in the early 1990s ford decided that  they were done with the i.t world so  they started looking for buyers for  their id division given that this was  right up bob's alley he made an offer  for 103 million dollars but he had one  condition he wanted the rights to use  ford's logos and branding for five years  now this was a pretty odd request but  bob was already running a respectable  software business and ford probably  thought that bob simply wanted some time  to rebrand the company so they agreed to  the deal but this turned out to be a big  mistake you see bob leveraged the trust  and respect dealers had for ford in  order to take an extremely predatory  deals he charged ludicrous amounts for  everyday tech this included charges such  as a twelve thousand dollar fee to  install a 500 megabyte hard drive and  2400 for a printer you would think that  dealers would have caught on to these  ridiculous fees but many of them didn't  look into the fine print and simply  trusted ford because the fine print was  a 12-inch thick contract when bob's  five-year license for ford's branding  was up though many of these dealers were  in for a rude awakening some of them  tried to rebel against bob alleging that  he had misled them but bob was well  prepared for this any complaint from the  dealers was already accounted for in the  fine print and the dealers couldn't just  wait it out given that these contracts  often extended decades so many of them  tried to go the litigation route by  suing bob but this just drowned them in  legal fees which were often just as bad  as bob's fees at the time it wasn't  really clear how bad these dealerships  were getting screwed dealers that  weren't affected simply dismissed the  concerns as they should have read the  fine print it wasn't until 10 to 15  years later that bob's damages finally  came to light as these dealerships began  to file for bankruptcy and shut down due  to his fees in 2010 for example a  californian entrepreneur named j jill  decided to expand his empire by  purchasing a group of 10 car dealerships  but when he looked into the financials  of the group he realized that the  company owed bob at 3 million and that  they were supposed to pay him at 35 000  every single month after seeing this jay  said quote i know unequivocally that i  wouldn't do business with this guy even  if it was free fortunately for jay he  didn't have to deal with bob for that  long as he agreed to settle for half  that amount but with fees like that it's  no wonder why car salesmen are so pushy  if they don't rip you off they can't  afford to get ripped off themselves  as you would guess the entire fiasco  with ford wasn't the best for ucs's  public reputation so bob started looking  to rebrand himself in the 2000s and the  perfect opportunity came in 2006 when he  got the opportunity to sell his company  to reynolds and reynolds similar to ucs  reynolds and reynolds had been selling  software to auto dealers since the 1980s  they had mostly grown their software  empire through a bunch of acquisitions  and offering to buy ucs was just another  one of their routine acquisitions i  don't think they did much due diligence  as a simple glance at bob's history  would have probably put them off  nevertheless reynolds and reynolds went  ahead and purchased ucs for 2.8 billion  dollars and as you would guess in  brockman fashion there was one critical  contingency for the acquisition bob  stipulated that he must be made ceo of  the merged company for the acquisition  to be completed reynolds agreed and it  didn't take long for bob to start  ruining their reputation as well first  of all he went ahead and implemented  some smaller rules that were annoying  but not too bad he refused to hire  smokers and forced all employees to get  an annual physical to maintain health  insurance some of his other policies  however were much more damaging about  one third of round's revenue was from  general motors to their integrated  dealership management program  considering how integral gm was to the  business you would think that bob would  treat them very well but in 2007 gm  ended up filing a lawsuit against  reynolds accusing them of refusing to  make requested changes to the software  reynolds ended up settling with gm a  year later which ended the gm  partnership somehow reynolds had already  lost their biggest customer just two  years after bob took over but bob didn't  care as he had his eye on a much bigger  price in 2007 a company called coindata  came out with a product called docupad  docupad is basically a large tabletop  tablet that eliminates a need for  paperwork when selling a car this makes  it much easier for car salesmen to  upsell customers on insurance programs  and amenities that they simply don't  need despite the appealing product most  car dealerships avoided the system given  that it was extremely expensive coin  data charged ten thousand dollars to set  up each system and then an additional 1  000 every month as a maintenance fee and  if a dealership wanted to modify  anything they were charged an extra 297  dollars for each modification coindata  had a tough time getting dealerships to  pick up these tabs but bob wasn't  worried about that in 2009 he offered to  form an exclusive marketing partnership  with coin data and over the next two  years they revamped the system in 2011  the duo came out with a new docupad and  with bob at the wheel these sold like  hotcakes it's still not clear if these  systems are actually worth it for  dealerships but there was no question  that they were worth it for bob  by this point bob was raking in billions  and though his business could be labeled  as immoral or unethical it definitely  wasn't illegal it would have been in  bob's best interest to keep it this way  but unfortunately it didn't take long  for greed to get to him in the late  1990s an investment banker from goldman  sachs named robert f smith reached out  to bob originally he offered to buy out  ucs from bob but bob turned the script  on robert bob offered to help robert  start up a new investment bank called  visa equity partners with 1 billion  dollars but in return bob wanted robert  to help him set up a foreign trust that  did not report to the irs technically  there were legal ways to accomplish this  and legitimate reasons to do so but i  think it was pretty clear that bob was  going to use this for illegal purposes  nonetheless this was an opportunity of a  lifetime for robert so he took up bob on  the offer the department of justice has  since labeled this deal as evidence of  conspiracy and scheme and artifice to  defraud so here's how the fraud worked  bob owned a charitable trust called  eugene brockman that was based in  bermuda this charitable trust on the  holding company called spanish steps  holding registered in nevis and spanish  steps holdings on the company called the  point investment which was registered in  the british virgin islands and the point  investments was investing in vista's  foreign fund bob used this convoluted  string of companies to funnel money  offshore and avoid taxes bob and robert  got away with this for decades but  eventually the irs caught on robert  didn't resist the irs much and quickly  turned on bob when he took in the  investment from bob he knew that this  could eventually happen and he was  prepared for it robert worked with the  irs to give them all the information  they needed and he happily paid a 139  million fine for his involvement in the  scheme from his perspective the deal  with bob was well worth it vista equity  partners had grown to 86 billion dollars  in assets under management which made  robert worth 8.9 billion dollars  considering this a 139 million fee  wasn't that bad as for bob his lawyers  tried to argue that he was suffering  from dementia and that he couldn't serve  jail time but quartz dismissed this  argument in november we don't know what  the final sentence is going to be but  it's probably not going to be that  forgiving at this point though it  doesn't really matter that much bob is  already 80 years old so he's already  avoided paying for his crimes for most  of his life aside from this the latest  account suggests that bob is worth 4.7  billion dollars so even if he was  charged a multi-hundred million dollar  fine or even a billion dollar fine it  wouldn't actually hurt him that much  financially really the worst punishment  he could get at this point is being  forced levi's last days in jail and that  might very well end up happening but  we'll just have to wait and see what do  you guys think should be bob's  punishment comment that down below also  drop a like if you're glad that bob gave  car dealers a taste of their own  medicine and of course consider checking  out our international channels to watch  our videos in other languages and  consider subscribing to see more  questions logically answered but until  then i'm hari and i'll see you guys on  the next one

